1. Update and reboot
bash
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
sudo apt install unattended-upgrades -y
sudo dpkg-reconfigure --priority=low unattended-upgrades
sudo reboot

Check for pending security fixes:

bash
pro security-status

Ubuntu Pro is free for personal use (up to 5 machines). It patches the Universe packages:

bash
sudo pro attach YOUR_TOKEN
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
2. Stop using root; use a sudo user
bash
sudo adduser "brown"
sudo usermod -aG sudo "user"

List users with sudo rights:

bash
getent group sudo

Remove sudo from an account (for example guest) or delete the account:

bash
sudo deluser guest sudo
sudo userdel -r guest

Test logging in as the new user before doing anything else. Optionally lock the root password afterwards:

bash
sudo passwd -l root
3. Passwords

Set or change a password:

bash
sudo passwd username

Find accounts with an empty password (this should print nothing):

bash
sudo awk -F: '($2 == "") {print $1}' /etc/shadow

Enforce complexity:

bash
sudo apt install libpam-pwquality -y
sudo nano /etc/security/pwquality.conf

Set:

minlen = 16
4. SSH keys

Run this on your client computer (not the server, not as root):

bash
ssh-keygen -t ed25519
ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub user@server-ip

Test the key login in a second terminal before continuing.

5. Harden SSH
bash
sudo nano /etc/ssh/sshd_config

Set:

PasswordAuthentication no
PermitRootLogin no
PermitEmptyPasswords no
PubkeyAuthentication yes
MaxAuthTries 3
AddressFamily inet

AddressFamily inet makes SSH use IPv4 only (optional).

Check for overrides. On Ubuntu 24.04, files in sshd_config.d can set PasswordAuthentication yes again:

bash
sudo grep -ri PasswordAuthentication /etc/ssh/

Check the syntax and restart:

bash
sudo sshd -t
sudo systemctl restart ssh

If [::]:22 still shows up in sudo ss -tlnp | grep :22, run sudo systemctl edit ssh.socket and add:

[Socket]
ListenStream=
ListenStream=0.0.0.0:22

Then:

bash
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart ssh.socket ssh
6. Disable IPv6 system-wide (optional)

Do not put these lines in sshd_config. They belong in sysctl:

bash
sudo nano /etc/sysctl.conf

Add at the end:

# disable IPv6 on the system
net.ipv6.conf.all.disable_ipv6=1
net.ipv6.conf.default.disable_ipv6=1

Apply and verify:

bash
sudo sysctl -p
cat /proc/sys/net/ipv6/conf/all/disable_ipv6     # should print 1

Then set IPV6=no in /etc/default/ufw and run sudo ufw reload.

7. FTP (vsftpd)

Best option: remove it and use SFTP (built into SSH):

bash
sudo apt remove --purge vsftpd

If you keep it, edit /etc/vsftpd.conf:

anonymous_enable=NO
local_enable=YES
write_enable=YES
local_umask=022
chroot_local_user=YES
allow_writeable_chroot=YES

# only listed users may log in
userlist_enable=YES
userlist_deny=NO
userlist_file=/etc/vsftpd.userlist

# encryption
ssl_enable=YES
force_local_logins_ssl=YES
force_local_data_ssl=YES
ssl_tlsv1=NO
ssl_tlsv1_1=NO
ssl_tlsv1_2=YES
require_ssl_reuse=NO

Delete these old lines:

anon_upload_enable=YES
anon_other_write_enable=YES
anon_mkdir_write_enable=YES
anon_root=/srv/ftp/uploads
anon_umask=000

Create the allowed-users list (never include guest) and restart:

bash
echo "user" | sudo tee /etc/vsftpd.userlist
sudo systemctl restart vsftpd
8. Samba (Windows file sharing)

Best option: remove it if you don't need it.

bash
sudo systemctl disable --now smbd nmbd
sudo apt remove --purge samba

If you keep it, edit /etc/samba/smb.conf:

Delete the whole [public] block. It shared / as root with no password.
Under [global], set:
map to guest = never
restrict anonymous = 2
usershare allow guests = no
server min protocol = SMB2
Safe example share:
[shared]
   path = /srv/samba/shared
   valid users = sheldon
   read only = no
   guest ok = no
bash
sudo mkdir -p /srv/samba/shared
sudo smbpasswd -a sheldon
testparm
sudo systemctl restart smbd
9. Firewall (UFW)

Add the SSH rule before enabling, or you will lock yourself out. SSH is port 22.

bash
sudo ufw default deny incoming
sudo ufw default allow outgoing
sudo ufw limit 22/tcp

Only if you run a web server:

bash
sudo ufw allow 80/tcp
sudo ufw allow 443/tcp

Block the risky services:

bash
sudo ufw deny 21/tcp
sudo ufw deny 139/tcp
sudo ufw deny 445/tcp

Enable and check:

bash
sudo ufw enable
sudo ufw status verbose

Stricter alternative for SSH (only your home network; adjust the subnet):

bash
sudo ufw allow from 192.168.1.0/24 to any port 22 proto tcp

Check what is listening (close anything you don't recognize):

bash
sudo ss -tulpn

Docker bypasses UFW. Publish ports as 127.0.0.1:8080:80 unless they must be public.

10. fail2ban
bash
sudo apt install fail2ban -y
sudo nano /etc/fail2ban/jail.local

Add:

[sshd]
enabled = true
maxretry = 5
bantime = 1h
bash
sudo systemctl enable --now fail2ban
sudo systemctl restart fail2ban
sudo fail2ban-client status sshd
11. Clean up
bash
sudo apt remove --purge debsecan postfix -y
sudo apt autoremove -y
12. Check for a past compromise

The old Samba [public] share gave anyone root access to / without a password, so check:

bash
cat /root/.ssh/authorized_keys ~/.ssh/authorized_keys     # keys you don't recognize
sudo crontab -l; ls -la /etc/cron.* /var/spool/cron/crontabs
sudo awk -F: '($3==0){print $1}' /etc/passwd              # should only print root
last -20
sudo lastb | head
ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head                                 # unknown CPU hogs
sudo find / -mtime -14 -type f -perm -4000 2>/dev/null     # recently changed SUID files

If you find signs of compromise, back up only your data and reinstall.

13. Audit and monitor
bash
sudo apt install lynis rkhunter -y
sudo lynis audit system
sudo rkhunter --update && sudo rkhunter --check

sudo snap install trivy
sudo trivy rootfs --severity HIGH,CRITICAL /
14. MySQL: make it local-only

If sudo ss -tlnp | grep mysqld shows 0.0.0.0:3306, MySQL is exposed on every interface.

bash
sudo nano /etc/mysql/mysql.conf.d/mysqld.cnf

Set:

bind-address = 127.0.0.1
bash
sudo systemctl restart mysql
sudo ss -tlnp | grep mysqld            # should show only 127.0.0.1
sudo ufw deny 3306/tcp

Check the database users (end every SQL command with ;; type \c to cancel a half-typed command):

bash
sudo mysql -e "SELECT user, host FROM mysql.user;"
sudo mysql -e "SELECT user, host FROM mysql.user WHERE authentication_string = '';"   # should return nothing

A user with host % can connect from anywhere; change it to localhost if it doesn't need remote access.

15. Kernel network hardening

This is what harden.sh applies. Create /etc/sysctl.d/90-hardening.conf:

net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter = 1
net.ipv4.conf.default.rp_filter = 1
net.ipv4.conf.all.accept_redirects = 0
net.ipv4.conf.default.accept_redirects = 0
net.ipv6.conf.all.accept_redirects = 0
net.ipv4.conf.all.send_redirects = 0
net.ipv4.conf.all.accept_source_route = 0
net.ipv4.tcp_syncookies = 1
net.ipv4.conf.all.log_martians = 1
kernel.kptr_restrict = 2
kernel.dmesg_restrict = 1

Apply it:

bash
sudo sysctl --system
16. Defending against exploitation and RCE

RCE (remote code execution) means an attacker makes a service such as a web server or database run commands for them.

Service accounts should not have login shells. The last field of each line in /etc/passwd is the shell, and for service accounts it should be /usr/sbin/nologin or /bin/false:

bash
grep -E '(www-data|nginx|apache|postgres|mysql|nobody)' /etc/passwd
sudo usermod -s /usr/sbin/nologin www-data
grep -vE 'nologin|/bin/false' /etc/passwd          # only root and real users should appear
awk -F: '$3==0 {print $1}' /etc/passwd             # should print only root

nologin blocks logins only. A reverse shell started through an RCE bug still runs, so treat it as one layer.

Reverse shell detection. A reverse shell is when the compromised machine connects out to the attacker:

bash
sudo ss -tunp state established
watch -n 2 'sudo ss -tunp state established'

Suspicious: bash, sh, nc, python or perl holding a connection to an external IP; nginx, apache2 or mysqld connecting out to an unknown IP; odd remote ports such as 4444 or 1337. Look up an IP with whois IP.

SUID / SGID auditing. SUID files run with the owner's privileges, and attackers use them to become root. Save a baseline now and compare later:

bash
sudo find / -xdev -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null | sort > ~/suid-baseline.txt
sudo find / -xdev -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null | sort | diff ~/suid-baseline.txt -
sudo find /tmp /var/tmp /dev/shm /home -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null     # should print nothing

Normal on Ubuntu: sudo, su, passwd, chsh, chfn, gpasswd, newgrp, mount, umount, pkexec, fusermount3. Dangerous: SUID on bash, find, vim, python, cp, nmap, less or awk, or any SUID file in a temp or home directory.

17. Automate it with harden.sh

harden.sh applies sections 1, 3, 5, 9, 10 and 15 and runs the checks from 12 and 16.

bash
sudo bash harden.sh --audit-only       # look first, changes nothing
sudo bash harden.sh                    # apply
sudo bash harden.sh --no-password-ssh  # only after key login works
